<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    HouseGraph daemon, as a macOS LaunchAgent.

    A LaunchAgent (~/Library/LaunchAgents), NOT a LaunchDaemon (/Library/LaunchDaemons).
    Graph execution still runs in the JavaFX app, which needs the logged-in GUI session — a
    LaunchDaemon runs before login with no window server and would fail on every start.
    See docs/engine/remote-runtime.md.

    Every path below must be absolute. launchd does not expand ~, and a job naming a path it
    cannot resolve fails to start with nothing useful in the log.

    Install:
        cp extras/launchd/com.jaymcole.housegraph.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
        # edit the paths below, then:
        launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.jaymcole.housegraph.plist

    Stop:
        launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.jaymcole.housegraph

    Restart (after replacing the jar):
        launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.jaymcole.housegraph

    Is it running?
        launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/com.jaymcole.housegraph

    The older `launchctl load`/`unload` spelling still works, but reports nearly every failure as
    "Load failed: 5: Input/output error" — including a plist that isn't where you think it is.
    bootstrap says what is actually wrong.

    The machine also needs automatic login enabled, and to stay awake:
        sudo pmset -a sleep 0 disablesleep 1
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.jaymcole.housegraph</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <!-- EDIT: the java binary to run under (java -version to check it's 21+). -->
        <string>/usr/bin/java</string>
        <!-- EDIT: the shaded jar built by ./gradlew :app:shadowJar. -->
        <string>-jar</string>
        <string>/Users/CHANGEME/HouseGraph/app-0.2.0.jar</string>
        <string>daemon</string>
    </array>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Restart the daemon itself if it ever exits. The daemon restarts individual graphs;
         this is the layer above, for the daemon process. It is also what makes selfUpdate
         work: installing a new HouseGraph release ends with the daemon exiting 10, and this
         is what starts it again on the jar it just installed. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Anything written before the logging pipeline is up (a JVM error, a missing jar) would
         otherwise be lost. HouseGraph's own log is separate, under its data directory —
         run `housegraph doctor` to see where. -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/CHANGEME/Library/Logs/housegraph-daemon.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/CHANGEME/Library/Logs/housegraph-daemon.log</string>

    <!-- Give a machine that has just booted a moment before the first sync: the network may not
         be up yet, and a failed first poll would just wait out the whole poll interval. -->
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>30</integer>

    <!-- How long launchd waits after SIGTERM before it SIGKILLs the daemon. It MUST exceed the
         daemon's own teardown budget, which is Supervisor.STOP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (40s) per graph,
         taken one graph at a time. launchd's default is 20 seconds — shorter than a single
         graph's budget — so without this key a restart kills the daemon part-way through
         stopping its children, orphaning the ones it had not reached. Those keep running, keep
         their ports, and the replacement graphs then fail to bind. This is the outermost layer
         of the timeout chain in docs/engine/node-lifecycle.md; raise it if you run many graphs
         or any that are slow to stop. -->
    <key>ExitTimeOut</key>
    <integer>120</integer>

    <!-- launchd starts a job with a near-empty environment: PATH is /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin
         and nothing your shell sets in ~/.zshrc is present. That is fine for HouseGraph itself,
         which needs only the java binary named above by absolute path, but NOT for nodes that
         shell out to a PATH-resolved launcher — a web node running `npm run build`, a Node-server
         node running `node server.js`. Those spawn `sh -c <command>`, which is non-interactive and
         so reads no profile either, and the command comes back "not found" (exit 127). It works
         when you start the daemon from a terminal, because the daemon inherits your shell's PATH
         and hands it to its children; under launchd there is nothing to inherit.

         The value below covers Homebrew on Apple Silicon (/opt/homebrew/bin) and on Intel
         (/usr/local/bin). Check yours with `which -a node npm` and add its directory if it lives
         somewhere else. A runtime managed by nvm has no stable path — nvm is a shell function,
         which launchd will never run — so on a server install node through Homebrew instead. -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
